---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = 'https://media.huanyuchen.com/misc/la-gardens/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Gardens, a Getty and a Battleship — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Afternoons around Los Angeles, 2021–2025: the Huntington's gardens three times, the USC Pacific Asia Museum, the Getty's cactus garden, the USS Iowa with Wangcai, a few streets at night and some December trees.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2021–2025</p>
  <h1>Gardens, a Getty and a Battleship</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Afternoons in Los Angeles: gardens, museums, a few streets at night, and the December trees.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>16 outings · October 2021 – June 2025</strong><br />
    The Huntington three times (October 2021, December 2021, March 2023) · the USC Pacific Asia Museum ·
    the Getty Center · the USS Iowa · CityWalk, Westwood, Hollywood Boulevard, Beachwood Canyon and Chinatown ·
    Christmas trees at the Grove, L.A. Live and the Citadel<br />
    One Chinese garden in three seasons · one battleship · and Wangcai, once, on the pier beside a battleship
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-la-gardens.webp'}><img src={P + 'map-la-gardens.webp'} alt="Map of the Los Angeles area marking the Huntington and the Pacific Asia Museum in the San Gabriel Valley, the Getty Center above the 405, CityWalk, the Grove, L.A. Live, the Citadel Outlets in Commerce, and the USS Iowa in San Pedro." /></a>
    <figcaption>Where the photos below were taken, from San Marino to San Pedro.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    This post is not about one trip. It collects the afternoons around Los Angeles that were spent somewhere other
    than a trail or a beach: gardens, museums, a few walks through town, and the big Christmas trees that go up every
    December. The outdoors and the coast have their own posts; so do the <a href="/misc/views-from-above">views from
    up high</a>, including Griffith Observatory. What is left is a smaller, quieter set, and most of it, it turns out,
    is the Huntington.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other posts here, everything is reconstructed from the camera roll: what was photographed, where, and
    at what minute.
  </p>

  <h2>A morning at the Pacific Asia Museum</h2>
  <p>
    The first of these outings, on Saturday, October 16, 2021, started in Pasadena at the <strong>USC Pacific Asia
    Museum</strong>. My first photo there is at 11:33 and the last at 12:36. The galleries that day had a samurai
    armor in a glass case, a folding screen painted with landscapes, blue-and-white porcelain, and a photo exhibition
    of the Hakka round houses, by Elizabeth Hill.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pam-armor.webp'} alt="A suit of black samurai armor with a horned gold helmet crest, standing in a tall glass case in a gallery." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Samurai armor, 11:37 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pam-porcelain.webp'} alt="A display case with a tall blue-and-white double-gourd vase, a pale green dish and a small white bowl." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Blue-and-white porcelain and a celadon dish.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last photo at the museum is of the pond in its courtyard: a curving stone rim, lily
    pads, and orange koi under the leaves.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pam-courtyard-pond.webp'} alt="A small pond with a curved stone edge, water lilies and orange koi, surrounded by grasses and ferns." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The courtyard pond, 11:57 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On the way to the Huntington there was a short stop at <strong>Pasadena City Hall</strong>, around 1:30, for the
    dome and its courtyard garden.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pasadena-city-hall.webp'} alt="The domed tower of Pasadena City Hall in white stone against a blue sky, with trees in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pasadena City Hall, 1:29 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>The Huntington, three times</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Huntington</strong> in San Marino is a library, an art museum and a set of botanical gardens on one
    large estate. It is also the place that appears most often in this post: in October 2021, again in December 2021,
    and once more in March 2023. All three visits ended up at the same lake in the Chinese garden, so the photos make
    a small record of one view in three seasons.
  </p>

  <h3>October 2021: the first visit</h3>
  <p>
    The first photos inside are from 2:13 pm. The first stop was the glass-domed <strong>conservatory</strong>,
    full of tropical leaves.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-conservatory.webp'} alt="Inside a glass-domed conservatory, packed with tropical plants, ferns and palms around a bench and terracotta pots." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The conservatory, 2:15 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From there the photos move to the <strong>Chinese garden</strong>, Liu Fang Yuan, which takes up most of the
    afternoon's camera roll. A lake sits in the middle, edged with rough, pitted rocks and crossed by a white stone
    bridge, with pavilions around the water. One of the pavilions had an exhibition of calligraphy, <em>A Garden of
    Words: The Calligraphy of Liu Fang Yuan</em>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-oct-lake.webp'} alt="A lake in a Chinese garden with rocky islands, green shrubs and pines along the shore, under a clear sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lake, 2:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-oct-bridge.webp'} alt="A white stone zigzag bridge across a lake, with an arched bridge behind it and trees on the far shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bridges, 3:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The photos from the next half hour go around the lake: past the bridges and the pavilions on the far shore,
    through a grove of bamboo, and up to a waterfall in the rocks.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-oct-pavilion.webp'} alt="A Chinese pavilion with a curved tiled roof among rocks and willows at the edge of the lake." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A pavilion by the water, 3:05 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Next door is the <strong>Japanese garden</strong>, and in it the bonsai court, where the trees are kept in pots and
    in low beds of gravel. This one, a wide pine with flat layers of needles, was photographed at 3:17.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-bonsai.webp'} alt="A wide pine trained into flat, cloud-like layers of needles, growing in a bed of gravel in front of a wooden wall." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>In the bonsai court, 3:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    By half past three the photos are in the <strong>Desert Garden</strong>: barrel cacti by the dozen, tall columns
    of cereus, and agaves under a hard blue sky. The last stop of the day was indoors, in the art collection, at 4:34,
    where Gainsborough's <em>The Blue Boy</em> hangs in a green room.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-desert-garden.webp'} alt="A bed of round golden barrel cacti beneath tall columnar cacti, beside a paved path." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Barrel cacti in the Desert Garden, 3:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-blue-boy.webp'} alt="A gilt-framed portrait of a boy in a blue satin suit, hanging on a green damask wall above a carved wooden chair." loading="lazy" /><figcaption><em>The Blue Boy</em>, 4:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>December 2021: winter</h3>
  <p>
    The second visit was on Wednesday, December 22, 2021, two months later. My first photo that day, at 1:23, is of the entrance, hung
    with Christmas wreaths. The sky was white and overcast this time.
    In the Japanese garden, a row of ginkgo trees along a plastered wall had turned completely yellow and dropped
    their leaves on the ground beneath them, and the slope below the Japanese house had a stone pagoda among red and
    orange shrubs.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-dec-ginkgo.webp'} alt="A row of slim ginkgo trees with bright yellow leaves along a long tan plastered wall, the ground covered in fallen yellow leaves." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ginkgo along the wall, 2:33 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-dec-pagoda.webp'} alt="A grey stone pagoda on a hillside of rocks and shrubs in red, orange and yellow, under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A stone pagoda in the Japanese garden, 2:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then back to the Chinese garden. The lake was the same as in October, but the water plants had died back to brown
    stalks, the willows were turning, and the pavilions on the far shore were reflected in flat grey water. Near the
    lake is a small waterfall over a wall of pitted grey rock.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-dec-lake.webp'} alt="A lake under an overcast sky, with brown lotus stalks in front and pavilions and willows reflected on the far shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same lake in December, 2:45 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-dec-waterfall.webp'} alt="A thin waterfall pouring down a wall of pitted grey limestone into a small pool, with shrubs around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The waterfall, 3:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On the way out, at 3:26, the photos pass the rose garden and the small round temple at its end, a ring of white
    columns under a dome.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-dec-temple.webp'} alt="A small round classical temple of white columns under a dome, at the end of a lawn with shrubs, under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The temple at the end of the rose garden, 3:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>March 2023: spring</h3>
  <p>
    The third visit, on Sunday, March 12, 2023, was an hour in the late afternoon, from about 3:40, mostly in the
    Chinese garden again. Six of the photos are mine, starting at 3:50. The first is a tree in full pink blossom. The rest are of the Chinese garden lake again, under a blue sky with
    white clouds this time, with the white arched bridge and a stone lantern standing in the water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-mar-blossom.webp'} alt="A small tree covered in pink blossom against a blue sky, with green shrubs and rocks below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Spring blossom, 3:50 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-mar-bridge.webp'} alt="A stone lantern standing in a lake, with a white arched bridge and willows behind, under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lantern and the bridge, 4:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Put next to the October and December photos, it is the same view three times: clear and green, grey and brown,
    and then blue and green again.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-mar-pavilion.webp'} alt="A Chinese pavilion with a dark curved roof on the lake shore, reflected in still green water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A pavilion on the lake, 4:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-mar-reflection.webp'} alt="A wide view of the lake under blue sky and white clouds, with the arched bridge, rocks and trees reflected in the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole lake, 4:03 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    My wife's photos from the same hour add two spring details: a double white blossom at 3:51, and an orange koi in
    the clear green water of the lake at 4:02.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-mar-white-blossom.webp'} alt="Close-up of double white blossoms and buds on a branch, with green leaves behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>White blossom, 3:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hunt-mar-koi.webp'} alt="An orange koi swimming in clear green water beside pale rocks." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A koi in the lake, 4:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>The Getty</h2>
  <p>
    On Saturday, February 24, 2024, we went to the <strong>Getty Center</strong>, on its hilltop above the 405 in
    Brentwood. Our photos from there are almost all of the view. To the east, a cluster of towers stands above the green of
    the Westside, with more on the horizon. Just below the terrace is the cactus garden,
    a round bed of spiky plants on a stone promontory.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'getty-dtla.webp'} alt="A wide view east over green neighborhoods to a cluster of high-rises, with more towers on the horizon under a pale blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking east from the Getty, 4:02 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'getty-cactus.webp'} alt="A curved stone promontory planted with cacti and succulents, seen from above, with the city spreading out beyond it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cactus garden, 4:04 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On the north side the hill drops into the Sepulveda Pass, and my wife's photo at 4:24 looks straight down it: the
    405 running through the gap between the hills, every lane full.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'getty-405.webp'} alt="A freeway packed with traffic running through a pass between green hills, under a blue sky with scattered clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The 405 in the Sepulveda Pass, 4:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>The USS Iowa, with Wangcai</h2>
  <p>
    On Saturday, June 14, 2025, we took Wangcai to San Pedro to see the <strong>USS Iowa</strong>, the battleship that
    is now a museum on the Los Angeles waterfront. The photos are all from outside, between 2:05 and 2:11, under a sky
    full of long streaky clouds: the grey superstructure, the big gun turrets, and the visitor gangway up to the deck.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'iowa-bow.webp'} alt="The grey battleship USS Iowa moored at a pier, its tall superstructure and gun turrets under streaky white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The USS Iowa, 2:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'iowa-gangway.webp'} alt="A ramp leading up to the battleship's deck, with a big gun turret above and a sign reading Iowa Visitor in the corner." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The visitor gangway, 2:07 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Wangcai was more interested in the pier. On the pier beside the ship lies a huge rusted anchor chain, and she stood and looked at it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'iowa-wangcai.webp'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a pink leash, seen from behind, looking at a huge rusty anchor chain lying on a concrete pier." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai and the anchor chain, 2:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In October 2026 she walked past another museum ship, the USS Midway, in <a href="/misc/san-diego">San Diego</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>In town</h2>
  <p>
    A few walks through town left only a handful of photos each. On a Monday night in November 2021 I walked
    <strong>Universal CityWalk</strong>, all neon and video screens: a popcorn shop with its name in lights, and the
    Universal Studios sign glowing over the entrance at 9:53, after a late film at the cinema there.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'citywalk-popcornopolis.webp'} alt="A red popcorn shop front with its name in looping neon script and glowing windows, at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>CityWalk, 7:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'citywalk-sign.webp'} alt="A neon Universal Studios sign in red, green and yellow over a blue-lit base, against a black night sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Universal Studios sign, 9:53 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Later that month, on the Friday after Thanksgiving, the white tower of the Village Theatre in
    <strong>Westwood</strong> was wrapped in a huge poster for <em>Licorice Pizza</em>, and the crowd was out on the
    crossing below it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'westwood-fox.webp'} alt="A white theater tower at night wrapped in a giant movie poster of a smiling woman, above a marquee reading Westwood Village and a busy crosswalk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Westwood Village, 6:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    In August 2022 there was a walk down <strong>Hollywood Boulevard</strong>, with a star on the Walk of Fame, and in
    April 2023 a drive up <strong>Beachwood Canyon</strong>, where the Hollywood Sign shows on the hill above
    the street.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hollywood-star.webp'} alt="A pink terrazzo star with a gold record emblem set in a black speckled sidewalk on the Walk of Fame." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A star on Hollywood Boulevard, August 2022.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'beachwood-sign.webp'} alt="A street lined with palms and parked cars, with the Hollywood Sign small on the hillside in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Beachwood Canyon, with the sign on the hill, April 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In July 2024 a short walk in <strong>Chinatown</strong> ended at Blossom Plaza, a red building with paper lanterns
    strung across its courtyard. And in March 2025 there was a public tour of NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, which
    left only a photo of the visitor badge.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chinatown-blossom-plaza.webp'} alt="A red multi-story building with balconies around a paved courtyard, with red paper lanterns hanging above and blue market tents below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Blossom Plaza in Chinatown, July 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>December trees</h2>
  <p>
    The last group is the Christmas trees. On the evening of December 21, 2021, I went with a group of friends to
    <strong>The Grove</strong>, where a tall tree covered in white and red lights stands in the middle of the outdoor
    mall, and a Santa's sleigh with reindeer hangs on a wire high above the fountain. The
    Grove's green trolley was running through the crowd, strung with lights for the season.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'grove-2021-tree.webp'} alt="A tall Christmas tree covered in white lights and red ornaments, with strings of lights overhead, at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Grove's tree, 8:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'grove-2021-sleigh.webp'} alt="Santa's sleigh and reindeer in lights hanging above a lit fountain, with the big Christmas tree glowing behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sleigh over the fountain, 8:38 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Three days later, on Christmas Eve, I walked past the tree at <strong>L.A. Live</strong> downtown, set up over an
    ice rink between the glass towers. A year after that, on December 23, 2022, the Grove's sleigh was up again, and
    this time I photographed it in the afternoon against a clear blue sky.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lalive-tree.webp'} alt="A tall dark-green Christmas tree beside an outdoor ice rink, surrounded by high-rise towers and a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>L.A. Live on Christmas Eve 2021, 1:26 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'grove-2022-sleigh.webp'} alt="Santa's sleigh and a line of reindeer hanging in the air against a blue sky, beside the top of a Christmas tree." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Grove by day, December 23, 2022, 3:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The most recent is at the <strong>Citadel Outlets</strong> in Commerce. We went on Thanksgiving night 2024,
    November 28. The tree there is lit in blue and purple, and the palms around it are wrapped in bright green lights,
    so the whole plaza glows green and violet.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'citadel-tree.webp'} alt="A very tall Christmas tree lit in blue and purple, with a green-lit palm beside it and a crowd at its base, at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Citadel tree, 8:21 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'citadel-palms.webp'} alt="Palm trees with their fronds wrapped in bright green lights, and the top of the purple Christmas tree behind them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Green palms around the tree, 8:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Christmas lights further south, in Laguna and Newport, are in <a href="/misc/irvine-oc">Irvine and around</a>.
  </p>
